from (values
('127.407.d.9.L',3,'A student team builds a water filter from sand, gravel, and charcoal, then measures turbidity before and after. The measurement serves to:','["rename the filter", "sell the filter", "test the filter''s performance", "decorate the filter"]'::jsonb,2,'Testing uses before-and-after data.','original'),
('127.407.d.9.L',4,'If a homemade filter lets too much sediment pass, the team should:','["declare success", "discard the lab report", "hide the data", "modify the layers and retest"]'::jsonb,3,'Design improves through iteration.','original'),
('127.407.d.9.M',3,'Phytoremediation uses living plants to:','["clean contaminated soil or water", "bake bread", "build roads", "generate noise"]'::jsonb,0,'Plants absorb or break down pollutants.','original'),
('127.407.d.9.M',4,'In a phytoremediation experiment, the control group should include a setup with:','["extra light", "no plants", "double the plants", "a different pollutant"]'::jsonb,1,'A control isolates the effect of plants.','original'),
('127.407.d.9.N',3,'In an experiment testing how temperature affects dissolved oxygen, the variable the experimenter changes is the:','["control group", "dependent variable", "independent variable", "conclusion"]'::jsonb,2,'The independent variable is manipulated.','original'),
('127.407.d.9.N',4,'To test whether fertilizer amount affects algae growth, a student should keep all other factors:','["random", "unknown", "different", "the same"]'::jsonb,3,'Controlled variables keep tests fair.','original'),
('127.407.d.10.A',4,'A nonrenewable energy source is one that:','["is used faster than it forms", "is free", "comes from wind", "never runs out"]'::jsonb,0,'Fossil fuels take millions of years to form.','original'),
('127.407.d.10.B',3,'Energy density describes how much energy a fuel stores per:','["hour of sunlight", "unit of mass or volume", "dollar of subsidy", "mile of road"]'::jsonb,1,'Energy density compares stored energy to size.','original'),
('127.407.d.10.B',4,'A government payment that lowers the price of an energy source is called a:','["tax audit", "tariff", "subsidy", "penalty"]'::jsonb,2,'Subsidies affect energy economics.','original'),
('127.407.d.10.C',3,'A student tracks monthly electricity use in kilowatt-hours. This measures:','["air quality", "water pressure", "noise level", "electrical energy used"]'::jsonb,3,'Kilowatt-hours measure electrical energy.','original'),
('127.407.d.10.C',4,'A household uses 30 kilowatt-hours per day. How much does it use in 30 days?','["900 kilowatt-hours", "300 kilowatt-hours", "60 kilowatt-hours", "9,000 kilowatt-hours"]'::jsonb,0,'30 x 30 = 900.','original'),
('127.407.d.10.D',3,'To compare the fuel efficiency of two fuels, engineers measure the energy output per:','["color of fuel", "unit of fuel burned", "year of fuel", "name of fuel"]'::jsonb,1,'Efficiency compares output to input.','original'),
('127.407.d.10.D',4,'A fuel that releases more energy per kilogram than another is considered:','["cheaper always", "unburnable", "more energy dense", "less useful"]'::jsonb,2,'Energy per mass defines density.','original'),
('127.407.d.10.E',3,'A simulation of a home''s energy use lets students test changes such as adding insulation before:','["learning math", "naming the house", "painting walls", "making real changes"]'::jsonb,3,'Models predict results at low cost.','original'),
('127.407.d.10.E',4,'When a simulation shows that adding solar panels cuts grid use by 40 percent, the student has analyzed:','["model output", "a rainbow", "a tax form", "a rumor"]'::jsonb,0,'Simulation results are data to interpret.','original'),
('127.407.d.10.F',3,'Solar and wind power are called intermittent because their output depends on:','["fuel deliveries", "weather and time of day", "worker schedules", "pipeline pressure"]'::jsonb,1,'Sun and wind are not constant.','original'),
('127.407.d.10.F',4,'An on-demand energy source, such as a natural gas plant, can:','["only work in wind", "only work at noon", "be turned up when needed", "never be controlled"]'::jsonb,2,'Dispatchable sources follow demand.','original'),
('127.407.d.10.G',3,'A life cycle assessment (LCA) evaluates a product''s environmental impact from raw materials through manufacture, use, and:','["designing", "advertising", "naming", "disposal"]'::jsonb,3,'LCA covers cradle to grave.','original'),
('127.407.d.10.G',4,'An LCA of two grocery bags would compare their impacts across:','["their entire life cycles", "only their colors", "only their names", "only their prices"]'::jsonb,0,'Full-life comparison is the point of an LCA.','original'),
('127.407.d.10.H',3,'An LCA must define its boundary, which sets:','["the company name", "which stages are included", "the color scheme", "the product price"]'::jsonb,1,'Boundaries scope the analysis.','original'),
('127.407.d.10.H',4,'A variable commonly measured in an LCA is the amount of:','["jokes in the manual", "paint on the box", "energy used at each stage", "pages in the report"]'::jsonb,2,'Energy inputs are inventoried.','original'),
('127.407.d.11.A',3,'The Clean Air Act helped improve U.S. air quality by setting limits on:','["coin minting", "school hours", "sports", "pollutant emissions"]'::jsonb,3,'The Act regulates air pollution sources.','original'),
('127.407.d.11.A',4,'The catalytic converter on a car reduces air pollution by converting harmful exhaust gases into:','["less harmful gases", "more smoke", "solid fuel", "metal dust"]'::jsonb,0,'Catalysts change toxic gases.','original'),
('127.407.d.11.B',3,'More frequent severe storms can threaten human habitats by damaging homes and:','["bakeries", "power infrastructure", "video games", "sports fields"]'::jsonb,1,'Storms disrupt energy access.','original'),
('127.407.d.11.B',4,'Extreme heat raises electricity demand for cooling, which can strain the:','["school bus", "post office", "power grid", "library"]'::jsonb,2,'Peak demand stresses the grid.','original'),
('127.407.d.11.C',3,'Raising a building above expected flood levels is an engineering strategy to make it more:','["decorative", "noisy", "temporary", "resilient"]'::jsonb,3,'Resilient design withstands extreme events.','original'),
('127.407.d.11.C',4,'A resilient power system might include backup generators and:','["multiple supply routes", "no maintenance", "a single wire", "one fuel truck"]'::jsonb,0,'Redundancy supports resilience.','original'),
('127.407.d.11.D',3,'Coastal wetlands increase natural resilience by absorbing:','["parking", "storm surge and floodwater", "music", "fireworks"]'::jsonb,1,'Wetlands buffer storms.','original'),
('127.407.d.11.D',4,'Healthy forests help natural resilience by reducing soil erosion and holding:','["snacks", "screens", "rainwater", "gold"]'::jsonb,2,'Forest soil absorbs rain.','original'),
('127.407.d.11.E',3,'The Air Quality Index (AQI) turns pollutant measurements into a:','["street address", "tax code", "song title", "single easy-to-read number"]'::jsonb,3,'AQI simplifies air data.','original'),
('127.407.d.11.E',4,'One city has an AQI of 40 and another has an AQI of 160. Which has worse air quality?','["the city with 160", "neither can be compared", "the city with 40", "both are equal"]'::jsonb,0,'Higher AQI means worse air.','original'),
('127.407.d.12.A',3,'A healthy ecosystem provides services such as clean water, pollination, and:','["printing", "climate regulation", "television", "traffic lights"]'::jsonb,1,'Ecosystems support human needs.','original'),
('127.407.d.12.A',4,'Biodiversity makes an ecosystem more stable because if one species declines, others can:','["stop growing", "multiply forever", "fill its role", "leave the planet"]'::jsonb,2,'Variety provides backups.','original'),
('127.407.d.12.B',3,'Farmland and timberland provide direct value by producing:','["advertising", "music", "insurance", "food and wood"]'::jsonb,3,'Direct production is a use value.','original'),
('127.407.d.12.B',4,'A forest that filters air and stores carbon provides an indirect value to people called an:','["ecosystem service", "parking permit", "entertainment fee", "property tax"]'::jsonb,0,'Indirect benefits are ecosystem services.','original'),
('127.407.d.12.C',3,'Restoring a damaged prairie by replanting native grasses is an example of land:','["paving", "restoration", "subdivision", "extraction"]'::jsonb,1,'Restoration repairs damaged land.','original'),
('127.407.d.12.C',4,'Leaving a wilderness area untouched so it stays as it is is called:','["mining", "restoration", "preservation", "development"]'::jsonb,2,'Preservation protects land from change.','original'),
('127.407.d.12.D',3,'Geographic information systems (GIS) and satellite images can be used to study how land cover changes:','["by accident only", "in music", "in secret", "over time"]'::jsonb,3,'Time series show land-use trends.','original'),
('127.407.d.12.D',4,'Comparing satellite images of a city taken 20 years apart can show how much farmland became:','["urban development", "space", "glaciers", "ocean"]'::jsonb,0,'Imagery shows land conversion.','original'),
('127.407.d.12.E',3,'A new highway can have a positive impact by easing traffic and a negative impact by:','["lowering noise", "fragmenting wildlife habitat", "cleaning rivers", "adding wildlife habitat"]'::jsonb,1,'Roads can split habitats.','original'),
('127.407.d.12.E',4,'When analyzing a development project, engineers report both positive and negative impacts in:','["a poem", "a menu", "an environmental impact assessment", "a bill"]'::jsonb,2,'Assessments document tradeoffs.','original'),
('127.407.d.12.F',3,'National parks and wildlife refuges are protected areas that help safeguard:','["parking lots", "factories", "shopping malls", "natural ecosystems"]'::jsonb,3,'Protected areas conserve nature.','original'),
('127.407.d.12.F',4,'A wildlife corridor between two protected areas helps animals to:','["move safely between habitats", "live without water", "stay in one cage", "avoid all food"]'::jsonb,0,'Corridors connect habitats.','original'),
('127.407.d.13.A',3,'Choosing a product with less packaging so that less trash is made is an example of waste:','["burial", "reduction", "recycling", "incineration"]'::jsonb,1,'Source reduction prevents waste.','original'),
('127.407.d.13.A',4,'Washing and refilling glass bottles instead of throwing them away is an example of:','["incineration", "disposal", "reuse", "dumping"]'::jsonb,2,'Reuse extends a product''s life.','original'),
('127.407.d.13.B',3,'If every student in a school stops using single-use water bottles, the school saves:','["sunlight", "gravity", "oxygen", "plastic and energy"]'::jsonb,3,'Less production means fewer resources used.','original'),
('127.407.d.13.B',4,'Composting food scraps at home reduces the waste sent to landfills and returns nutrients to:','["the soil", "the atmosphere", "the pipes", "the ocean floor"]'::jsonb,0,'Compost enriches soil.','original'),
('127.407.d.13.C',3,'A modern landfill collects the liquid that drains through waste, called:','["brine fuel", "leachate", "effluent gas", "compost tea"]'::jsonb,1,'Leachate is treated to protect groundwater.','original'),
('127.407.d.13.C',4,'Landfill gas collection systems capture methane so it can be burned or used to make:','["paper", "plastic", "energy", "soil"]'::jsonb,2,'Methane is a fuel.','original'),
('127.407.d.13.D',3,'Which waste breaks down fastest in a compost pile?','["aluminum cans", "plastic bags", "glass bottles", "banana peels"]'::jsonb,3,'Organic waste decomposes quickly.','original'),
('127.407.d.13.D',4,'Plastic bags can last for hundreds of years in the environment because they are:','["not easily broken down by microbes", "dissolved by rain", "eaten by fish", "made of food"]'::jsonb,0,'Plastics resist decomposition.','original'),
('127.407.d.13.E',3,'Used batteries, paint, and pesticides are examples of waste that is:','["edible", "hazardous", "recyclable only", "harmless"]'::jsonb,1,'They contain toxic materials.','original'),
('127.407.d.13.E',4,'Hazardous waste should go to a special collection site rather than down a drain because it can:','["feed plants", "improve plumbing", "contaminate soil and water", "clean pipes"]'::jsonb,2,'Improper disposal spreads pollution.','original'),
('127.407.d.14.A',3,'The federal agency that writes and enforces rules such as clean air and clean water standards is the:','["Postal Service", "Weather Channel", "Federal Reserve", "Environmental Protection Agency"]'::jsonb,3,'The EPA regulates pollution.','original'),
('127.407.d.14.A',4,'The U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service is responsible for protecting:','["endangered species and habitats", "highways", "bank deposits", "television signals"]'::jsonb,0,'USFWS manages wildlife conservation.','original')
