('127.407.d.14.B',3,'In Texas, permits for wastewater discharges and air emissions are issued by the:','["Texas Workforce Commission", "Texas Commission on Environmental Quality", "Texas Lottery Commission", "Texas Education Agency"]'::jsonb,1,'The TCEQ issues environmental permits.','original'),
('127.407.d.14.B',4,'The Texas agency that manages state parks and fish and wildlife resources is the:','["Texas Department of Insurance", "Texas Railroad Commission", "Texas Parks and Wildlife Department", "Texas Rangers"]'::jsonb,2,'TPWD protects state natural resources.','original'),
('127.407.d.14.C',3,'Under the National Environmental Policy Act (NEPA), major federal projects must be reviewed through an:','["tax return", "sports schedule", "advertising plan", "environmental impact statement"]'::jsonb,3,'NEPA requires impact review.','original'),
('127.407.d.14.C',4,'NEPA requires federal agencies to consider environmental effects:','["before making decisions", "only if asked", "never", "after finishing projects"]'::jsonb,0,'Review comes before action.','original'),
('127.407.d.14.D',3,'In Texas, the Railroad Commission oversees the restoration of lands disturbed by:','["farming only", "surface coal mining", "school building", "parades"]'::jsonb,1,'The TRC regulates mine reclamation.','original'),
('127.407.d.14.D',4,'Reclamation of a mined site typically includes regrading the land and replanting:','["parking meters", "power lines", "vegetation", "glass"]'::jsonb,2,'Reclamation restores land cover.','original'),
('127.407.d.15.A',3,'Climate change is a persistent challenge partly because its causes and solutions involve:','["only chemistry", "no data", "one small town", "many countries and industries"]'::jsonb,3,'Global problems need broad cooperation.','original'),
('127.407.d.15.A',4,'Environmental problems like groundwater contamination can persist because pollutants may remain for:','["decades", "minutes", "hours", "seconds"]'::jsonb,0,'Some contaminants break down very slowly.','original'),
('127.407.d.15.B',3,'A student proposes rain barrels and permeable pavement to reduce a school''s runoff. This is creating a:','["new law of physics", "solution to a current challenge", "lunch menu", "school holiday"]'::jsonb,1,'Engineers design solutions for society.','original'),
('127.407.d.15.B',4,'A good solution to a community problem must meet people''s needs and also:','["avoid testing", "hide data", "limit harm to the environment", "ignore costs"]'::jsonb,2,'Sustainable solutions balance needs.','original'),
('127.407.d.15.C',3,'A resilient solution should be able to withstand a disruption and then:','["disappear", "double in cost", "collapse", "recover quickly"]'::jsonb,3,'Resilience means bouncing back.','original'),
('127.407.d.15.C',4,'Building redundancy into a system, such as a second pump, supports resilience by:','["providing a backup if one part fails", "doubling the noise", "removing safeguards", "avoiding all maintenance"]'::jsonb,0,'Backups keep systems running.','original'),
('127.407.d.15.D',3,'The energy used to mine materials, make a product, ship it, and dispose of it is part of its:','["sales tax", "life cycle energy cost", "shelf color", "brand value"]'::jsonb,1,'Life cycle analysis totals energy use.','original'),
('127.407.d.15.D',4,'Describing a product''s life cycle starts with extraction of raw materials and ends with:','["marketing", "design", "disposal or recycling", "naming"]'::jsonb,2,'The life cycle ends at end of life.','original'),
('127.408.d.1',3,'A pump engineer discovers a design flaw that could cause a hydraulic line to burst near workers. Ethics requires the engineer to:','["keep it secret", "wait for an injury", "blame the operator", "report the flaw and act to fix it"]'::jsonb,3,'Safety duties come first.','original'),
('127.408.d.1',4,'An engineer''s professional code says the safety of the public must be held:','["paramount", "irrelevant", "secondary", "optional"]'::jsonb,0,'Public safety outranks other concerns.','original'),
('127.408.d.2.A',3,'A student sees that water drains faster from a tall tank than a short one and asks why. This question comes from:','["a law", "an observation", "a rumor", "a budget"]'::jsonb,1,'Questions start with observations.','original'),
('127.408.d.2.A',4,'Defining the problem ''reduce pump energy use by 15 percent'' gives the team a:','["slogan", "guess", "measurable goal", "hobby"]'::jsonb,2,'Goals with numbers can be tested.','original'),
('127.408.d.2.B',3,'Measuring how fast three different oils flow down a ramp is a comparative investigation because it:','["avoids measuring", "has no result", "uses no oil", "compares results among samples"]'::jsonb,3,'Comparative studies contrast samples.','original'),
('127.408.d.2.B',4,'In an experiment on flow rate, the variable the investigator measures as the result is the:','["dependent variable", "control rod", "independent variable", "constant"]'::jsonb,0,'The dependent variable is the outcome.','original'),
('127.408.d.2.C',3,'When working with a pressurized hydraulic test rig, a student should wear:','["sandals", "safety glasses", "headphones only", "a scarf"]'::jsonb,1,'Eye protection guards against spray.','original'),
('127.408.d.2.C',4,'A student notices fluid leaking from a lab pump. The correct first step is to:','["keep working", "step over the puddle", "stop the pump and tell the instructor", "touch the leak"]'::jsonb,2,'Stop the hazard and report it.','original'),
('127.408.d.2.D',3,'To measure the inside diameter of a small pipe fitting precisely, a student could use:','["a stopwatch", "a thermometer", "a bathroom scale", "dial calipers"]'::jsonb,3,'Calipers measure small dimensions.','original'),
('127.408.d.2.D',4,'A graduated cylinder is the right tool for measuring the:','["volume of a liquid", "angle of a bend", "length of a pipe", "speed of a car"]'::jsonb,0,'Graduated cylinders measure liquid volume.','original'),
('127.408.d.2.E',3,'A student records 750 milliliters of water. In liters, this is:','["75 liters", "0.75 liters", "0.075 liters", "7.5 liters"]'::jsonb,1,'750 / 1,000 = 0.75.','original'),
('127.408.d.2.E',4,'A pipe is 36 inches long. In feet, its length is:','["30 feet", "12 feet", "3 feet", "0.3 feet"]'::jsonb,2,'36 / 12 = 3.','original'),
('127.408.d.2.F',3,'A student records the number of seconds a funnel takes to drain each trial in a spreadsheet. These are:','["opinions", "theories", "qualitative data", "quantitative data"]'::jsonb,3,'Measured numbers are quantitative.','original'),
('127.408.d.2.F',4,'Notes such as ''the oil looked cloudy and thick'' are:','["qualitative observations", "unit conversions", "calculated results", "numerical measurements"]'::jsonb,0,'Descriptions are qualitative.','original'),
('127.408.d.2.G',3,'A drawing of a water tower with arrows showing the flow of water is a:','["law", "model of a system", "unit", "hypothesis"]'::jsonb,1,'Diagrams model how systems work.','original'),
('127.408.d.2.G',4,'A computer simulation of water flowing through a pipe network is a model that helps predict:','["prices", "weather", "flow behavior", "grades"]'::jsonb,2,'Simulations predict system behavior.','original'),
('127.408.d.2.H',3,'A statement predicting that thicker oil will flow more slowly, which can be tested, is a:','["unit", "summary", "law", "hypothesis"]'::jsonb,3,'A hypothesis is a testable prediction.','original'),
('127.408.d.2.H',4,'A scientific law, such as conservation of mass, differs from a hypothesis because a law has been:','["supported by extensive evidence", "guessed once", "never tested", "voted on"]'::jsonb,0,'Laws rest on repeated confirmation.','original'),
('127.408.d.3.A',3,'A small scale model of a spillway is cheaper to test, but its flow may not match the real spillway''s because of:','["price effects", "scale effects", "name effects", "paint effects"]'::jsonb,1,'Scaling can change fluid behavior.','original'),
('127.408.d.3.A',4,'A model that simplifies a pipe network by ignoring friction will likely:','["underestimate the flow", "stop working", "overestimate the flow", "be exact"]'::jsonb,2,'Friction slows real flow.','original'),
('127.408.d.3.B',3,'A graph of flow rate against pipe diameter slopes upward. This pattern shows that larger pipes carry:','["no flow", "less flow", "colder flow", "more flow"]'::jsonb,3,'Flow increases with diameter.','original'),
('127.408.d.3.B',4,'To judge how consistent repeated flow measurements are, an analyst can calculate their:','["standard deviation", "weight", "color", "shape"]'::jsonb,0,'Standard deviation measures spread.','original'),
('127.408.d.3.C',3,'A pump delivers 90 gallons in 6 minutes. Its flow rate is:','["96 gallons per minute", "15 gallons per minute", "0.07 gallons per minute", "540 gallons per minute"]'::jsonb,1,'90 / 6 = 15.','original'),
('127.408.d.3.C',4,'If the flow rate is multiplied by 2 while the pipe area stays the same, the fluid velocity:','["halves", "triples", "doubles", "stays the same"]'::jsonb,2,'Velocity = flow rate / area.','original'),
('127.408.d.3.D',3,'After a test shows a new nozzle design sprays unevenly, the team evaluates the design by comparing results with its:','["lunch plans", "trophy list", "paint colors", "performance criteria"]'::jsonb,3,'Evaluation compares data to criteria.','original'),
('127.408.d.3.D',4,'An experiment that changes two variables at once makes causes hard to separate, so a test should change:','["one variable at a time", "every variable", "no variables", "variables at random"]'::jsonb,0,'Controlled tests isolate effects.','original'),
('127.408.d.4.A',3,'A student claims a wider drain empties a tank faster and supports it with timed trials. The claim is stronger because it is backed by:','["opinion", "measured data", "luck", "popularity"]'::jsonb,1,'Evidence strengthens claims.','original'),
('127.408.d.4.A',4,'A proposed solution to a clogged pipe should be supported by data and a model showing that it will:','["add more clogs", "hide the pipe", "restore the intended flow", "slow every pump"]'::jsonb,2,'Solutions must meet the goal.','original'),
('127.408.d.4.B',3,'A team explains its pump design with a diagram, a data table, and a short talk. They are communicating:','["in secret", "with no evidence", "only by guessing", "in several ways"]'::jsonb,3,'Multiple formats help audiences.','original'),
('127.408.d.4.B',4,'When a team presents its results, listeners can best follow if the explanation uses clear labels and:','["correct units", "jokes", "silence", "slang"]'::jsonb,0,'Units make numbers meaningful.','original'),
('127.408.d.4.C',3,'A respectful way to challenge a classmate''s flow calculation is to:','["call them careless", "point to the step you think is wrong and show why", "ignore the work", "interrupt loudly"]'::jsonb,1,'Argue the ideas, not the person.','original'),
('127.408.d.4.C',4,'A strong scientific argument about a fluid test includes a claim, evidence, and:','["a joke", "a prize", "reasoning", "an insult"]'::jsonb,2,'Reasoning links evidence to the claim.','original'),
('127.408.d.5.A',3,'To critique a report claiming a new coating reduces drag, a reader checks whether the report includes:','["bright colors", "a long title", "a famous author", "data from controlled tests"]'::jsonb,3,'Evaluate the evidence.','original'),
('127.408.d.5.A',4,'A claim based on a single trial of a wind-tunnel test is weak because the result might be:','["a random fluke", "always exact", "a law", "impossible"]'::jsonb,0,'Repeated trials build confidence.','original'),
('127.408.d.5.B',3,'Bernoulli''s work in the 1700s on fluid motion has influenced modern:','["sports rules", "airplane wing design", "poetry", "bakery recipes"]'::jsonb,1,'His principle relates speed and pressure in fluids.','original'),
('127.408.d.5.B',4,'Research on streamlined shapes has changed engineering by helping vehicles use less:','["time zones", "space only", "fuel", "steel only"]'::jsonb,2,'Reduced drag saves energy.','original'),
('127.408.d.5.C',3,'A student interested in fluid engineering careers could learn more from a professional group such as the:','["Bureau of Fashion", "National Football League", "Society of Pastry Chefs", "American Society of Mechanical Engineers"]'::jsonb,3,'Professional societies share career information.','original'),
('127.408.d.5.C',4,'Visiting a water treatment plant or hydropower station lets students observe:','["fluid systems in real use", "only paperwork", "unrelated jobs", "no equipment"]'::jsonb,0,'Field visits show applications.','original'),
('127.408.d.6.A',3,'Large volumes of liquid fuel are efficiently moved across the country by:','["bicycles", "pipelines", "backpacks", "kites"]'::jsonb,1,'Pipelines transport fluids efficiently.','original'),
('127.408.d.6.A',4,'Storing fluids in tanks with a rounded or cylindrical shape helps them to:','["fall over easily", "float away", "withstand pressure evenly", "hold no fluid"]'::jsonb,2,'Curved walls distribute stress.','original'),
('127.408.d.6.B',3,'In a hydroelectric dam, moving water turns a:','["mirror", "candle", "balloon", "turbine"]'::jsonb,3,'The turbine drives a generator.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
