('127.408.d.6.C',4,'The upward force on a wing that helps an airplane stay in the air is:','["gravity", "drag", "lift", "friction only"]'::jsonb,2,'Lift counters weight.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.A',3,'A fluid differs from a solid because it takes the shape of its:','["label", "scale", "magnet", "container"]'::jsonb,3,'Fluids flow to fill their container.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.A',4,'Which of these is a fluid?','["motor oil", "wooden plank", "granite block", "steel beam"]'::jsonb,0,'Liquids and gases are fluids.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.B',3,'A fluid whose viscosity stays constant at a given temperature regardless of shear rate is called:','["magnetic", "Newtonian", "brittle", "crystalline"]'::jsonb,1,'Water and air behave as Newtonian fluids.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.B',4,'Ketchup, which gets thinner when shaken, is an example of a fluid that is:','["gaseous", "solid", "non-Newtonian", "Newtonian"]'::jsonb,2,'Its viscosity changes with shear.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.C',3,'Which fluid is usually treated as incompressible?','["steam", "air", "helium", "liquid water"]'::jsonb,3,'Liquids change volume very little under pressure.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.C',4,'Gases are generally considered compressible because their:','["density changes noticeably with pressure", "color changes", "mass disappears", "names change"]'::jsonb,0,'Gas volume shrinks under pressure.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.D',3,'Density is defined as mass per unit:','["charge", "volume", "length", "time"]'::jsonb,1,'Density = mass / volume.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.D',4,'Specific gravity compares a liquid''s density to the density of:','["steel", "sand", "water", "air"]'::jsonb,2,'Water is the reference for liquids.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.E',3,'A student measures a liquid with mass 500 g in 400 mL. The density is:','["200 g/mL", "0.8 g/mL", "100 g/mL", "1.25 g/mL"]'::jsonb,3,'500 / 400 = 1.25.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.E',4,'A hydrometer floats in a liquid to directly measure the liquid''s:','["specific gravity", "temperature", "length", "pressure"]'::jsonb,0,'Hydrometers read specific gravity.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.F',3,'A fluid has density 800 kg/m3. Its specific gravity relative to water (1,000 kg/m3) is:','["800", "0.8", "1.25", "8"]'::jsonb,1,'800 / 1,000 = 0.8.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.F',4,'Specific weight equals density times g. For water at 1,000 kg/m3 and g = 9.8 m/s2, the specific weight is about:','["98 N/m3", "98,000 N/m3", "9,800 N/m3", "980 N/m3"]'::jsonb,2,'1,000 x 9.8 = 9,800.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.G',3,'Following a single parcel of fluid as it moves through a pipe uses which viewpoint?','["random frame", "Eulerian (fixed) frame", "Newtonian frame", "Lagrangian (material) frame"]'::jsonb,3,'Lagrangian frames move with the material.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.G',4,'Watching how fluid velocity changes at a fixed point in a pipe uses which viewpoint?','["Eulerian (spatial) frame", "static frame", "Galilean frame", "Lagrangian (material) frame"]'::jsonb,0,'Eulerian frames stay fixed in space.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.H',3,'In the ideal gas law PV = nRT, the variable T stands for:','["time", "absolute temperature", "torque", "thickness"]'::jsonb,1,'T must be in kelvin.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.H',4,'If the temperature of a gas in a rigid container rises, its pressure:','["decreases", "stays zero", "increases", "disappears"]'::jsonb,2,'Pressure is proportional to temperature at fixed volume.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.I',3,'In steady flow, the law of conservation of mass says the mass flow rate into a pipe equals the mass flow rate:','["doubled inside it", "destroyed inside it", "created inside it", "leaving it"]'::jsonb,3,'Mass in equals mass out at steady state.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.I',4,'The law of conservation of energy says that energy in a fluid system can change form but cannot be:','["created or destroyed", "measured", "transferred", "converted"]'::jsonb,0,'Total energy is conserved.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.J',3,'The no-slip condition says that fluid touching a solid wall has the same velocity as:','["the sound", "the wall", "the ambient air only", "the center of the pipe"]'::jsonb,1,'Fluid sticks to the surface.','original'),
('127.408.d.7.J',4,'Because of the no-slip condition, fluid velocity in a pipe is lowest:','["at the inlet only", "at the center", "at the pipe wall", "everywhere equally"]'::jsonb,2,'Velocity is zero at a stationary wall.','original'),
('127.408.d.8.A',3,'A force of 200 N acts on an area of 0.5 m2. The pressure is:','["0.0025 Pa", "100 Pa", "200 Pa", "400 Pa"]'::jsonb,3,'P = F/A = 200/0.5 = 400.','original'),
('127.408.d.8.A',4,'If the same force is applied over a smaller area, the pressure:','["increases", "becomes zero", "stays the same", "decreases"]'::jsonb,0,'Pressure is inversely proportional to area.','original'),
('127.408.d.8.B',3,'In a hydraulic jack, a small piston of area 2 cm2 is pushed with 50 N. The large piston has area 20 cm2. The output force is:','["50 N", "500 N", "5,000 N", "5 N"]'::jsonb,1,'Force scales with the area ratio of 10.','original'),
('127.408.d.8.B',4,'In a hydraulic system, pressure applied to a confined fluid is transmitted:','["only upward", "only to the largest piston", "equally in all directions", "not at all"]'::jsonb,2,'This is Pascal''s law.','original'),
('127.408.d.8.C',3,'Absolute pressure equals gauge pressure plus:','["specific gravity", "zero", "vapor pressure only", "atmospheric pressure"]'::jsonb,3,'Gauge pressure is measured relative to the atmosphere.','original'),
('127.408.d.8.C',4,'A tire gauge reads 32 psi. If atmospheric pressure is about 14.7 psi, the absolute pressure is about:','["46.7 psi", "470 psi", "32 psi", "17.3 psi"]'::jsonb,0,'32 + 14.7 = 46.7.','original'),
('127.408.d.8.D',3,'A Bourdon tube pressure gauge works because a curved tube tends to:','["melt", "straighten when pressurized", "change color", "freeze"]'::jsonb,1,'The tube''s motion moves the needle.','original'),
('127.408.d.8.D',4,'A manometer measures pressure by comparing the heights of:','["two wires", "two clocks", "a liquid column", "a battery"]'::jsonb,2,'Height difference reflects pressure.','original'),
('127.408.d.8.E',3,'According to Archimedes'' principle, the buoyant force on an object equals the weight of the:','["air above", "container", "object itself", "fluid displaced"]'::jsonb,3,'Buoyancy equals displaced fluid weight.','original'),
('127.408.d.8.E',4,'A block displaces 3 liters of water (about 3 kg). The buoyant force is about:','["29.4 N", "9.8 N", "300 N", "3 N"]'::jsonb,0,'3 x 9.8 = 29.4.','original'),
('127.408.d.8.F',3,'Hydrostatic pressure in a still fluid increases with:','["color", "depth", "width", "speed"]'::jsonb,1,'Deeper fluid supports more weight above.','original'),
('127.408.d.8.F',4,'Using P = rho x g x h, the pressure from a 10 m column of water (rho = 1,000 kg/m3, g = 9.8 m/s2) is:','["980,000 Pa", "980 Pa", "98,000 Pa", "9,800 Pa"]'::jsonb,2,'1,000 x 9.8 x 10 = 98,000.','original'),
('127.408.d.9.A',3,'Fluid flow whose velocity profile no longer changes along the pipe is called:','["chaotic only", "stationary", "just started", "fully developed"]'::jsonb,3,'Developed flow has a constant profile.','original'),
('127.408.d.9.A',4,'Steady-state flow means that at any point, fluid properties do not change with:','["time", "pipe size", "position", "fluid type"]'::jsonb,0,'Steady means time-independent.','original'),
('127.408.d.9.B',3,'In laminar flow in a round pipe, the velocity profile is:','["flat", "parabolic", "spiky", "square"]'::jsonb,1,'Velocity is highest at the center.','original'),
('127.408.d.9.B',4,'An idealized uniform velocity profile assumes all fluid across the pipe moves at:','["random speeds", "different speeds", "the same speed", "zero speed"]'::jsonb,2,'Uniform means equal velocity.','original'),
('127.408.d.9.C',3,'A line that shows the path a single fluid particle follows over time is a:','["baseline", "timeline", "contour", "pathline"]'::jsonb,3,'Pathlines trace particle motion.','original'),
('127.408.d.9.C',4,'Injecting dye continuously from one point in a flow produces a:','["streakline", "gridline", "skyline", "shoreline"]'::jsonb,0,'Streaklines mark all particles from one point.','original'),
('127.408.d.9.D',3,'Water flows at 2 m/s through a pipe with a cross-sectional area of 0.05 m2. The volumetric flow rate is:','["0.025 m3/s", "0.1 m3/s", "2.05 m3/s", "40 m3/s"]'::jsonb,1,'Q = vA = 2 x 0.05 = 0.1.','original'),
('127.408.d.9.D',4,'By the continuity equation, when a pipe narrows, the fluid speed in the narrow section:','["reverses", "stays the same", "increases", "decreases"]'::jsonb,2,'Smaller area means higher speed.','original'),
('127.408.d.9.E',3,'Bernoulli''s equation relates pressure, velocity, and:','["pipe age", "label", "color", "elevation"]'::jsonb,3,'It balances pressure, kinetic, and potential energy.','original'),
('127.408.d.9.E',4,'For a horizontal pipe with steady flow, where fluid speed is higher, the pressure is:','["lower", "infinite", "higher", "unchanged"]'::jsonb,0,'Faster flow has lower pressure.','original'),
('127.408.d.9.F',3,'Fluid enters a pipe at 1 m/s through area A and then flows through a section of area A/2. The speed there is:','["0.5 m/s", "2 m/s", "1.5 m/s", "4 m/s"]'::jsonb,1,'Halving the area doubles the speed.','original'),
('127.408.d.9.F',4,'A pipe''s diameter is halved. The cross-sectional area becomes:','["one-half", "unchanged", "one-fourth", "double"]'::jsonb,2,'Area depends on the square of diameter.','original'),
('127.408.d.9.G',3,'Torricelli''s equation gives the exit speed v = sqrt(2gh) from a hole in a tank. If the water level h is raised, the exit speed:','["reverses", "stays the same", "decreases", "increases"]'::jsonb,3,'Higher head gives faster outflow.','original'),
('127.408.d.9.G',4,'Using v = sqrt(2gh) with g = 10 m/s2 and h = 20 m, the exit speed is:','["20 m/s", "40 m/s", "200 m/s", "10 m/s"]'::jsonb,0,'sqrt(2 x 10 x 20) = sqrt(400) = 20.','original'),
('127.408.d.9.H',3,'The Reynolds number compares inertial forces to:','["magnetic forces", "viscous forces", "sound forces", "gravity only"]'::jsonb,1,'Re = inertia / viscosity.','original'),
('127.408.d.9.H',4,'A flow with a low Reynolds number is most likely:','["turbulent", "supersonic", "laminar", "frozen"]'::jsonb,2,'Low Re means smooth flow.','original'),
('127.408.d.9.I',3,'A free jet of water leaves a nozzle and strikes a flat plate. The force on the plate depends on the jet''s mass flow rate and its:','["temperature", "color", "label", "velocity"]'::jsonb,3,'Force relates to momentum change.','original'),
('127.408.d.9.I',4,'By Newton''s laws, the force a fluid jet exerts on a plate equals the rate of change of the jet''s:','["momentum", "temperature", "color", "volume only"]'::jsonb,0,'Force = rate of change of momentum.','original'),
('127.408.d.10.A',3,'Smooth flow in which fluid moves in parallel layers is called:','["turbulent flow", "laminar flow", "static flow", "compressed flow"]'::jsonb,1,'Laminar flow is orderly.','original'),
('127.408.d.10.A',4,'Flow with eddies and chaotic mixing is called:','["laminar flow", "sonic flow", "turbulent flow", "still flow"]'::jsonb,2,'Turbulent flow is irregular.','original')
