('127.408.d.10.B',3,'For flow in a pipe, a Reynolds number above about 4,000 generally indicates:','["frozen flow", "laminar flow", "no flow", "turbulent flow"]'::jsonb,3,'High Re means turbulence.','original'),
('127.408.d.10.B',4,'Water (density 1,000 kg/m3, viscosity 0.001 Pa s) moves at 0.1 m/s in a 0.02 m pipe. Re = (rho v D)/mu equals:','["2,000", "20,000", "200", "0.002"]'::jsonb,0,'1,000 x 0.1 x 0.02 / 0.001 = 2,000.','original'),
('127.408.d.10.C',3,'Lift is the force on a body in a fluid that acts:','["parallel to the flow", "perpendicular to the flow", "toward the sun", "in all directions equally"]'::jsonb,1,'Lift is perpendicular to the oncoming flow.','original'),
('127.408.d.10.C',4,'Drag is the force on a body in a fluid that acts:','["toward the center", "without direction", "in the direction opposite its motion", "upward only"]'::jsonb,2,'Drag opposes motion.','original'),
('127.408.d.10.D',3,'Viscosity is a fluid''s resistance to:','["freezing", "being weighed", "compression only", "flow or shearing"]'::jsonb,3,'Viscosity measures internal friction.','original'),
('127.408.d.10.D',4,'Honey is more viscous than water, so for the same shear rate it requires:','["greater shear force", "no shear force", "less shear force", "negative force"]'::jsonb,0,'Shear stress = viscosity x shear rate.','original'),
('127.408.d.10.E',3,'The drag force on a body depends on fluid density, the body''s frontal area, its drag coefficient, and the square of its:','["color", "speed", "label", "name tag"]'::jsonb,1,'Drag grows with the square of velocity.','original'),
('127.408.d.10.E',4,'If a vehicle''s speed doubles, the drag force becomes about:','["half as large", "the same", "four times as large", "twice as large"]'::jsonb,2,'Drag varies with speed squared.','original'),
('127.408.d.10.F',3,'A wind-tunnel test to measure the drag coefficient of a model car would record drag force, air speed, and:','["the tunnel''s color", "the day of the week", "the car''s price", "the frontal area"]'::jsonb,3,'Cd = 2F / (rho v^2 A).','original'),
('127.408.d.10.F',4,'To compare the drag coefficients of a sphere and a streamlined shape fairly, the tests should use the same:','["fluid and air speed", "paint", "color", "names"]'::jsonb,0,'Controlled conditions give fair comparisons.','original'),
('127.408.d.11.A',3,'Flow at low speed, such as water in a pipe, is usually modeled as incompressible because its density:','["disappears", "stays nearly constant", "varies wildly", "doubles"]'::jsonb,1,'Density barely changes at low speeds.','original'),
('127.408.d.11.A',4,'At high speeds, air must be treated as compressible because its density:','["is zero", "is fixed", "changes significantly", "stays frozen"]'::jsonb,2,'Compressibility matters near the speed of sound.','original'),
('127.408.d.11.B',3,'For fluids with the same stiffness, a denser fluid generally has a speed of sound that is:','["infinite", "higher", "zero", "lower"]'::jsonb,3,'Speed of sound is sqrt(stiffness / density).','original'),
('127.408.d.11.B',4,'The speed of sound in air is about 343 m/s, and in water about 1,480 m/s, because water is much:','["less compressible", "more compressible", "colder", "lighter"]'::jsonb,0,'Stiffer fluids carry sound faster.','original'),
('127.408.d.11.C',3,'Mach number is the ratio of an object''s speed to the:','["speed of the wind only", "speed of sound", "speed of a car", "speed of light"]'::jsonb,1,'Ma = v / a.','original'),
('127.408.d.11.C',4,'A flow at Mach 0.2 is usually modeled as:','["hypersonic", "supersonic", "incompressible", "compressible"]'::jsonb,2,'Below Mach 0.3, density change is small.','original'),
('127.408.d.11.D',3,'When an aircraft exceeds the speed of sound, it creates:','["static", "rainbows", "magnets", "shock waves"]'::jsonb,3,'Pressure waves pile into a shock.','original'),
('127.408.d.11.D',4,'A sonic boom is heard when the shock wave from a supersonic aircraft:','["reaches the ground", "is canceled by wind", "turns into light", "is absorbed completely by space"]'::jsonb,0,'Observers hear the passing shock.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.A',3,'A weir placed across an open channel is used to:','["heat water", "measure or control flow", "increase noise", "add color"]'::jsonb,1,'Weirs regulate and gauge flow.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.A',4,'The water depth above the crest of a weir is used to estimate the:','["water color", "channel age", "flow rate", "fish count"]'::jsonb,2,'Head over the weir relates to discharge.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.B',3,'In open-channel flow, the hydraulic radius is the cross-sectional area divided by the:','["flow speed", "water depth only", "channel length", "wetted perimeter"]'::jsonb,3,'Rh = A / P.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.B',4,'If a channel is tilted to a steeper slope, the flow velocity generally:','["increases", "decreases", "stays zero", "reverses"]'::jsonb,0,'Steeper channels flow faster.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.C',3,'A ship floats when the buoyant force on its hull equals the ship''s:','["cargo list", "weight", "length", "color"]'::jsonb,1,'Floating requires force balance.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.C',4,'A designer makes a model boat hull wider so it sits higher. A larger submerged volume gives more:','["friction", "weight", "buoyant force", "drag"]'::jsonb,2,'Displacing more water raises buoyancy.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.D',3,'A clepsydra measures time using the steady flow of water through a small opening in a:','["lever", "pendulum", "candle", "vessel"]'::jsonb,3,'Water clocks track draining or filling.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.D',4,'A water tower provides pressure to a city''s pipes mainly by storing water at a high:','["elevation", "temperature", "color", "salinity"]'::jsonb,0,'Height produces hydrostatic pressure.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.E',3,'A positive displacement pump moves fluid by trapping a fixed amount and:','["freezing it", "forcing it through the outlet", "letting it float", "painting it"]'::jsonb,1,'It pushes fixed volumes per cycle.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.E',4,'Gear pumps and piston pumps are examples of:','["wind turbines", "centrifugal pumps", "positive displacement pumps", "water towers"]'::jsonb,2,'They move fixed volumes.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.F',3,'A centrifugal pump adds energy to a fluid using a spinning:','["lever", "piston", "valve", "impeller"]'::jsonb,3,'The impeller accelerates fluid outward.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.F',4,'A pump performance curve typically plots head against:','["flow rate", "age", "color", "price"]'::jsonb,0,'Curves show head versus capacity.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.G',3,'A basic hydraulic system includes a reservoir, a pump, valves, an actuator, and:','["a spring only", "hydraulic fluid", "compressed gas only", "a fuse"]'::jsonb,1,'The fluid carries pressure through the circuit.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.G',4,'In a hydraulic circuit, the component that converts fluid pressure into linear motion is the:','["filter", "hose", "cylinder (actuator)", "reservoir"]'::jsonb,2,'Cylinders produce straight-line motion.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.H',3,'Turbomachines transfer energy between a fluid and a rotating:','["pipe", "magnet", "wire", "rotor"]'::jsonb,3,'Rotors exchange energy with the fluid.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.H',4,'A machine that extracts energy from flowing fluid to turn a shaft is a:','["turbine", "pump", "filter", "valve"]'::jsonb,0,'Turbines drive shafts.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.I',3,'A jet engine uses a compressor and a turbine; the compressor''s job is to:','["cool the pilot", "raise the air pressure", "filter fuel", "burn the fuel"]'::jsonb,1,'Compression precedes combustion.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.I',4,'A wind turbine and a hydroelectric turbine both convert fluid motion into:','["light only", "sound only", "rotating shaft power", "heat only"]'::jsonb,2,'Both drive generators through a shaft.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.J',3,'Tribology is the study of friction, wear, and:','["music", "weather", "finance", "lubrication"]'::jsonb,3,'Tribology covers interacting surfaces.','original'),
('127.408.d.12.J',4,'Adding oil between two sliding metal surfaces reduces:','["friction and wear", "pressure only", "density only", "weight"]'::jsonb,0,'Lubricants separate surfaces.','original'),
('127.407.d.10.A',3,'Hydroelectric power from flowing rivers is classified as which kind of energy source?','["fossil-based", "renewable", "nonrenewable", "extinct"]'::jsonb,1,'Rivers are replenished by the water cycle.','original')
